INT. SALT MARSH RELAY STATION - DAY

A low adobe hut crouched among white crusted flats where nothing grows. Inside: a plank table, a rusted stove, a calendar from three years ago. Heat shimmers through a single dirty window. Outside, a lone horse stands tied to a post, head hanging.

ROSALIE FENN, forties, the station keeper, wears a man's hat and a woman's pride. A widow's ring on a chain around her neck. She is scrubbing a tin plate when the door slams open.

THADDEUS "TAD" McCREADY, thirties, black duster gray with dust, a bandage dark with blood at his shoulder, falls across the threshold with a pistol in his fist.

TAD
Nobody move.

ROSALIE
There's nobody to move, mister.

TAD
(to the empty room)
I said nobody move!

He swings the pistol around the hut, corner to corner, finds only a cat on the sill. Then Rosalie.

ROSALIE
You're bleeding on my floor.

TAD
Where's the stage?

ROSALIE
Gone through at noon. Won't be back till Thursday.

TAD
Thursday.

ROSALIE
(calmly)
It's Tuesday. Put the gun down and sit.

TAD
(muttering)
Lord help me.

He doesn't put it down. His hand wobbles. Sweat runs into his eyes. She sets the plate on the table and slowly dries her hands on her apron.

ROSALIE
I've seen men bleed. I've seen men run. I've never seen one do both and keep hold of a gun.

TAD
You calling me a fool?

ROSALIE
I'm calling you a patient. Sit.

The pistol drops to his side. He sits. The chair shrieks. She pulls a bottle from under the stove and pours into a cracked cup.

ROSALIE (CONT'D)
Drink.

He does. His shoulders loosen. She kneels to unwind the bandage.

TAD
There's three men behind me.

ROSALIE
Then we'll want to be quick.

She works. The cat watches. Outside, the horse snorts and stamps once, and a thin line of dust rises on the horizon.

ROSALIE (CONT'D)
(to herself)
Always three.

She ties off the bandage and takes a rifle down from the rafters.
